### Step 2 : Extracting Data by Month

when we use `ppp` , it only accepts numeric or character marks. However currently we only have the date (incident_datetime) by a date format. Therefore, we will have to extract out the `Month_num` (1 to 12 from January to December) and `Month_fac` to produce readable facet labels without inline conversion.

`DayofYear` is also extracted for now in case of the need for analysis of each specific day of the year.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
acc <- acc %>%
  mutate(
    DayofYear = yday(incident_datetime),
    Month_num = month(incident_datetime),
    Month_fac = month(incident_datetime, label = TRUE, abbr = FALSE)
  )
```
:::


### Step 3 : Visualising accidents by month

Before computing the KDE, it is important to plot a faceted point map (one small map of Thailand per month).

This is a first order visualisation of the data to understand the spatial distribution of accidents across the year and if there is any seasonality before computing the KDE. It also acts as the baseline before the STKDE is done to allow a comparison to be done.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
tm_shape(thailand_sf) +
  tm_polygons() +
  tm_shape(acc) +
  tm_dots(size = 0.1) +
  tm_facets(by = "Month_fac",
            free.coords = FALSE,
            drop.units = TRUE)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](Take-Home_ex01_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-17-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


::: callout-important
**Analysis of plot.**

From the plot, it can be seen that April and December have a heavier distribution throughout the months in the year, which ties in the monthly trends in Thailand with higher vehicle numbers on the road from the [Songkran Holiday travelling in April](https://he02.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/agstjournal/article/view/275930) and New Year Holiday travels in December.

However it is to note that in all 12 months, there is a darker concentration around the Bangkok and central provinces proving that Bangkok area is a permanent high-risk accident zone. In contrast, the North-Eastern Region in Thailand is showing fewer dots in the visuals throughout the year reflecting a lower accident volume assuming that the data provided is fully representative of all accidents that occurred in 2022 (as mentioned in assumptions made).
:::

### Step 4 : coverting to ppp

Since we are looking at the Month specifically we will keep only the Month number column and use it to convert to ppp.

It is also recommended to check again here to see if there are duplicated units. Usually this is done to prevent any inaccuracies when analysing data. However in this case, it is expected for there to be multiple crashes at the same intersection so it's fine for there to be duplicate values. The presence of duplicate values if any violates the strict independence assumption of point pattern analysis, but the events are retained because they represent genuinely distinct accidents. Therefore, the duplicate check is just to note that there are these sort of multi-crash accidents, and highlight the presence of this analytical limitation when conducting the analysis.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Keep only Month_num column
acc_month <- acc %>% select(Month_num)

# Convert to ppp
acc_month_ppp <- as.ppp(acc_month)

# Check for duplicates 
any(duplicated(acc_month_ppp))
```

