The `spattemp.density()` function has estimated a Spatial bandwidth of 38159.91 (\~38.16km). This is with the smoothing distance in UTM Zone 47N metres. It is known that a larger `h` produces a smoother, more generalised surface while a smaller `h` reveals local hot spots. Overall it is decided that the spatial bandwidth should not be further changed it balances detail against noise given the irregular shape of Thailand's boundary.

The temporal bandwidth is estimated to be lambda = 0.0079 (in units of the temporal range). Since time was rescaled to `[1, 12]` months, we have a narrow temporal smoothing window of about 0.1 month, which means that the density surface will change sharply month-to-month which is best to emphasize the high intensity time scope of April and December. Therefore edge correction is applied to reduce the bias near Thailand's border, where points outside the boundary cannot be observed but still contribute to the intensity estimate.

The trivariate density is normalised and conditioned to allow each monthly slice to integrate to 1 and make for direct visual comparison across months.

