In usual circumstances, it is possible to directly convert the OWIN objects to ppp using ppp\[owin\]. However the current Thailand boundaries are defined using very detailed polygons with many vertices in the bounary rings. Thus it is required to simplify the OWIN to ensure that the computation is properly reduced. The analytical results below will not be changed.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
#Simplification of OWIN object using st_simplify 
make_simple_owin <- function(sf_obj, tol_m) {
  sf_obj %>%
    st_make_valid() %>%
    st_simplify(dTolerance = tol_m, preserveTopology = TRUE) %>%
    st_union() %>%
    st_zm(drop = TRUE, what = "ZM") %>%
    as.owin()
}

bmr_owin   <- make_simple_owin(bmr$sf,   tol_m = 2000)
north_owin <- make_simple_owin(north$sf, tol_m = 3000)
ne_owin    <- make_simple_owin(ne$sf,    tol_m = 3000)
# now we can convert to ppp 
acc_bmr_ppp   <- acc_ppp[bmr_owin]
acc_north_ppp <- acc_ppp[north_owin]
acc_ne_ppp    <- acc_ppp[ne_owin]
```
