#### Simplification of ppp

As mentioned in the previous step when creating the ppp in Task 2, the current Thailand boundaries are defined using very detailed polygons with many vertices in the boundary rings. So the OWIN is simplified to ensure that the computation is properly reduced. The analytical results below will not be changed.

### Limitations/Caveats

#### Reduction of nrval and subsampling in specified regions

The Bangkok Metropolitian region analysis will uses the standard `nrval` (100) and subsampling (1000) values , which is well-suited to the renrvallatively compact BMR window of `rmax` =23.58km. However, the Northern and North-Eastern regions with larger, more elongated boundary windows that are bounded by more complex administrative boundaries, the isotropised correction would be computationally complex to run within R studio with the standard `nrval` and subsampling definitions. Therefore as a counter the the `nrval` for the Northern and North Eastern regions are reduced to 50 and subsampling is reduced to 500.

#### Duplicated Coordinates

As mentioned in Task 1, there are multiple accidents that has occurred at the same location. However, Ripley's K assumes events are independent, so the multiple accidents ;exact duplicates violate this assumption by artificially inflating short-range clustering. The duplicates were retained because each record represents a genuinely distinct accident, but the resulting K-curve at very short distances (below \~1 km) should be interpreted with this limitation in mind.

#### Biasedness in Ripley Correction

Ripley's isotropised edge correction is used throughout. For irregular windows in this context, the isotropised correction has known bias at larger distances as the search circle extends further beyond the boundary. The `rmax` bound limits this, but some residual bias may persist near the upper end of the evaluation range.

### Bangkok Metropolitan Region

First, the set seed is added in to ensure that the points stay the same in every calculation for easier analysis. Then we use the rescaled ppp variables to be put into the Ripley correction to get the best values before we do out the K Function plot for the Bangkok Metropolitan Region.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
set.seed(1234)
acc_bmr_small <- acc_bmr_ppp.km[sample(npoints(acc_bmr_ppp.km), 1000)]
K_bmr <- Kest(acc_bmr_small, correction = "Ripley")
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
plot(K_bmr, . -r ~ r, ylab= "K(d)-r", xlab = "d(m)")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](Take-Home_ex01_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-36-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


::: callout-note
Analysis :

The edge-corrected `K̂_iso(r) − r` for the Bangkok Metropolitan Region accidents are plotted against the theoretical CSR expectation (red dashed line). The observed edge corrected curve lies consistently above the CSR line with a widening gap up to `rmax = 23.58 km`, which shows a clustering of accidents rather than spatial randomness that is consistent with the Clark-Evans result (R = 0.265, p \< 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶), which identified strong nearest-neighbour clustering.

Formal significance of the deviation across all distances is assessed in the next section using a global envelope test.
:::

Now we will have to conduct the Complete Spatial Randomness Test to confirm the observed spatial patterns above. To do this, a hypothesis test will be conducted, The hypothesis test will be put under a 5% envelope where p\<0.05 for the results to be statistically significant (nsim =39 with this setting).

Ho = The distribution of accidents at the Bangkok Metropolitan Region are randomly distributed.

H1= The distribution of accidents at the Bangkok Metropolitan Region are not randomly distributed.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
#A 5% global CSR envelope (nsim = 39, rank = 1), evaluated at 100 distances up to rmax = 23.5 km.
K_bmr.csr <- envelope(acc_bmr_small, Kest,
                      nsim = 39, rank = 1, global = FALSE,
                      rmax = 23.5, nrval = 100)
```

