This confirms that the accidents in the Bangkok Metropolitan Region does exhibit statistically significant spatial clustering beyond what complete spatial randomness would produce which again is consistent with the Clark-Evans test (R = 0.265, p \< 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶), which identified strong nearest-neighbour clustering.

Together, the two tests have proven that the accidents in the Bangkok Metropolitan Region are not uniformly distributed but concentrate along a spatial structure which most plausibly the urban road network from the Bangkok subregion plot where there are highly intensive highlighted regions on the highways of Bangkok.
:::

